== 1 Introduction <sec:introduction>

A group factor is built from the multiplication of a discrete group, but an isomorphism of factors need not preserve its distinguished group unitaries. We ask when the group, a scalar twist of its multiplication, and the given factor isomorphism can all be recovered. For the lattice class below, we prove this recovery even after amplification and with an arbitrary ICC comparison group.

Connes proved countability of the fundamental group of a property-$\( T \)$ group factor #cite(label("Connes1980")); his later problems ask whether nonisomorphic ICC property-$\( T \)$ groups have nonisomorphic factors, and ask for the fundamental groups of those factors #cite(label("Connes1994")) Chapter V, Appendix B.$epsilon$, Problems 1–2. The stronger stable formulation displayed by Popa asks for recovery of the given isomorphism up to a character and inner conjugacy, with amplification scale one #cite(label("Popa2007")) Section 3, Equations (3.2) and (3.2$'$).

The proof uses the more general relation of finite correspondence: two factors act normally and commute on one Hilbert space, with finite module dimensions on both sides. We determine these correspondences through isomorphisms between finite-index subgroups and finite-dimensional projective representations. The representation records the ratio of the two scalar cocycles on the matched subgroups. Neither cocycle separately need have a finite-dimensional realization.

Our algebraic calculation identifies every bounded map, summand, and composition of these arithmetic models for arbitrary countable ICC groups. The geometric exhaustion theorem of the companion #cite(label("ArithmeticCompanion")) Theorem 1.2 makes the models exhaustive for the lattice class. Their integral dimensions recover stable isomorphisms; their compatibility with multiplication also recovers arbitrary finite-index factor neighbors.

=== 1.1 The common conventions and lattice class <the-common-conventions-and-lattice-class>

#prodef([Definition 1.1.])[
  Let $cal(K)$ be the class of countably infinite ICC groups abstractly commensurable with a lattice in a finite nonempty product
  $ G = product_(i = 1)^r G_i \, #h(2em) G_i = upright(bold(H))_i \( k_i \)^(+) . $
  Here $k_i$ is $bb(R)$, $bb(C)$, or a finite extension of $bb(Q)_p$; the primes may differ. The algebraic group $upright(bold(H))_i$ is connected, adjoint, and absolutely simple over $k_i$. The superscript $+$ denotes the subgroup generated by the $k_i$-points of the unipotent radicals of proper $k_i$-parabolic subgroups. Each $G_i$ is required to be noncompact and to have Kazhdan’s property $\( T \)$.
  A lattice is discrete and has finite Haar covolume. Abstract commensurability means an actual isomorphism between finite-index subgroups. ICC means that every nonidentity conjugacy class is infinite. No irreducibility, cocompactness, product decomposition, or torsion hypothesis is imposed on the lattice.
] <def:class>

For example, $upright("SL")_3 \( bb(Z) \)$ and every finite-index subgroup belong to $cal(K)$. Section 5 verifies the lattice and ICC hypotheses for these and other named examples.

Property $\( T \)$ means that every continuous unitary representation with almost invariant unit vectors has a nonzero invariant vector. Standard structure and permanence results for this class are recalled in Section 2; see also #cite(label("BHV2008")).

All scalar cocycles take values in $bb(T)$ and are normalized:

$ mu \( g \, h \) mu \( g h \, j \) = mu \( h \, j \) mu \( g \, h j \) \, #h(2em) mu \( e \, g \) = mu \( g \, e \) = 1 . $

The twisted group factor $L_mu \( Gamma \)$ has canonical unitaries $u_g$ with $u_g u_h = mu \( g \, h \) u_(g h)$ and trace $tau \( u_g \) = upright(bold(1))_({ e }) \( g \)$. For an ICC group it is a factor; Section 2 recalls the short Fourier argument.

A bifinite $M$–$N$ correspondence is a Hilbert space with commuting normal unital left $M$ and right $N$ actions and finite von Neumann module dimensions on both sides. The actions need not be mutual commutants, and the correspondence need not be irreducible. Every correspondence in this paper is separable; every factor isomorphism is normal, unital, complex-linear, and $*$-preserving. For a finite factor $Q$, write

$ Q^t = p #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] M_n \( bb(C) \) thin overline(⊗) thin Q #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] p \, #h(2em) \( "Tr"_n ⊗ tau_Q \) \( p \) = t > 0 . $

The matrix trace $"Tr"_n$ is unnormalized; the corner itself has its normalized trace. The fundamental group of $Q$ is $cal(F) \( Q \) = { t > 0 : Q^t tilde.equiv Q }$.

=== 1.2 Stable canonical recovery <stable-canonical-recovery>

A stable comparison is an isomorphism between positive amplifications of two factors. The following theorem forces the amplification scales to agree and determines the given map on its actual matrix corners.

#prodef([Theorem 1.2.])[
  (Presentation equivalence and stable canonical recovery).  #emph[Let $Lambda in cal(K)$ and let $Pi$ be any countably infinite ICC group. Let $omega \, nu$ be arbitrary normalized scalar cocycles on them, and let $t \, s > 0$. Then 
$ L_omega \( Lambda \)^t tilde.equiv L_nu \( Pi \)^s $

 if and only if $t = s$ and there are an isomorphism $delta : Lambda arrow.r Pi$ and a normalized cochain $b : Lambda arrow.r bb(T)$ such that 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.1)")
 $ omega \( g \, h \) = b \( g \) b \( h \) b \( g h \)^(- 1) nu \( delta \( g \) \, delta \( h \) \) . $ <eq:intro-cochain>]

 Every specified isomorphism at unit scale has the form 
$ theta \( u_g \) = w thin b \( g \) v_(delta \( g \)) w^(*) $

 for a unitary $w$ in the target.]
  #emph[More explicitly, put $M = L_omega \( Lambda \)$ and $N = L_nu \( Pi \)$, and let 
$ theta : p M_n \( M \) p arrow.r q M_m \( N \) q $

 be a specified isomorphism, where the unnormalized traces of $p$ and $q$ are $t$ and $s$. Then $t = s$, and one may choose $delta \, b$ as above and $z in M_(m \, n) \( N \)$ so that, for the canonical map $phi \( u_g \) = b \( g \) v_(delta \( g \))$, 
$ z^(*) z = phi^(\( n \)) \( p \) \, #h(2em) z z^(*) = q \, #h(2em) theta \( x \) = z phi^(\( n \)) \( x \) z^(*) . $

 Whenever such an isomorphism exists, the recovered comparison group $Pi$ belongs to $cal(K)$. In particular every factor $L_omega \( Lambda \)$ in this class has trivial fundamental group.]
] <thm:stable>

The untwisted specialization answers the stable recovery question for Definition 1, with an arbitrary ICC comparison group. Property $\( T \)$ alone does not imply this recovery. OpenAI constructed a countably infinite family of pairwise nonisomorphic ICC property-$\( T \)$ groups with isomorphic group factors #cite(label("OpenAITenAdvances2026")) Chapter 4, Theorem 1.2. Independently and concurrently, Zhou constructed another pair #cite(label("Zhou2026")) Theorem A.

=== 1.3 Arbitrary finite-index factor neighbors <arbitrary-finite-index-factor-neighbors>

We next allow the second factor to have no given group presentation. A #emph[finite-index factor neighbor] of $M$ is a factor joined to $M$ by a nonzero bifinite correspondence. The arithmetic data describing such a neighbor involve finite-index subgroup isomorphisms rather than an isomorphism of the whole groups.

For a group $A$, a normalized projective unitary representation with multiplier $beta$ is a map $sigma : A arrow.r cal(U) \( V \)$ satisfying $sigma \( a \) sigma \( b \) = beta \( a \, b \) sigma \( a b \)$ and $sigma \( e \) = I$. We call $beta$ #emph[finite type] when such a representation exists with $0 < dim_(bb(C)) V < oo$; its image need not be finite. For a group homomorphism $delta$, the pullback cocycle is $delta^(*) omega \( a \, b \) = omega \( delta \( a \) \, delta \( b \) \)$.

#prodef([Theorem 1.3.])[
  (All factor endpoints).  #emph[Let $Gamma in cal(K)$, let $mu$ be any normalized scalar cocycle on $Gamma$, and let $P$ be a $upright("II")_1$ factor with separable predual. There is a nonzero bifinite $L_mu \( Gamma \)$–$P$ correspondence if and only if 
$ P tilde.equiv L_omega \( Lambda \)^t $

 for a countably infinite ICC group $Lambda$, a normalized scalar cocycle $omega$ on $Lambda$, and $t > 0$, for which there are finite-index subgroups $A lt.eq Gamma$ and $B lt.eq Lambda$, an actual isomorphism $delta : A arrow.r B$, and a nonzero finite-dimensional projective unitary representation of $A$ with multiplier 
$ frac(mu \|_(A times A), delta^(*) \( omega \|_(B times B) \)) . $

 In particular, the reconstructed group $Lambda$ belongs to $cal(K)$.]
] <thm:endpoints>

The unknown factor $P$ is not assumed to have a group presentation or a preferred basis. Its presentation is recovered from multiplication in a finite-index extension. If $mu = 1$, finite-index projective induction recovers the usual finite-type condition on $omega$. For arbitrary $mu$ the ratio condition is essential: $P = L_mu \( Gamma \)$ is always an endpoint.

=== 1.4 The arithmetic correspondence category <the-arithmetic-correspondence-category>

The two recovery theorems use a common classification of correspondences. We first describe its models and then the finite-dimensional data that control their maps and summands.

For finite-index subgroups $A lt.eq Gamma$ and $B lt.eq Lambda$, an isomorphism $delta : A arrow.r B$, and a nonzero finite-dimensional projective unitary representation $sigma$ satisfying

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.2)")
 $ sigma \( a \) sigma \( b \) = frac(mu \( a \, b \), omega \( delta \( a \) \, delta \( b \) \)) sigma \( a b \) \, #h(2em) sigma \( e \) = I \, $ <eq:intro-multiplier>]

define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.3)")
 $ E \( A \, B \, delta \, sigma \) = L^2 \( M \) thin overline(⊗)_(L_(mu \|_A) \( A \)) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] V_sigma ⊗ L^2 \( N \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \, #h(2em) M = L_mu \( Gamma \) \, quad N = L_omega \( Lambda \) . $ <eq:intro-model>]

The right $N$ action is standard. The left subgroup action on the second factor is $u_a \( xi ⊗ eta \) = sigma \( a \) xi ⊗ v_(delta \( a \)) eta$. Equation (1.2) makes this an action with multiplier $mu \|_A$.

A right action is represented by $rho \( h \) xi = xi v_h^(*)$, so $rho \( h \) rho \( k \) = overline(omega \( h \, k \)) rho \( h k \)$. The left action is $pi \( g \) xi = u_g xi$. Together they give the joint action $U_(\( g \, h \)) xi = u_g xi v_h^(*)$.

A #emph[virtual-isomorphism germ] from $Gamma$ to $Lambda$ is an isomorphism between finite-index subgroups, modulo agreement on a common finite-index subgroup. The action of $Gamma times Lambda$ on germs is

$ \( g \, h \) dot.op \[ delta \] = \[ "Ad" \( h \) compose delta compose "Ad" \( g^(- 1) \) \] . $

For a germ $d$ let $C_d$ denote its full stabilizer. ICC implies that $C_d$ is the graph of an isomorphism $A_d arrow.r B_d$ between finite-index subgroups; the proof is included in Section 3. Use the joint multiplier

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.4)")
 $ alpha #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( g \, h \) \, \( g' \, h' \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = mu \( g \, g' \) overline(omega \( h \, h' \)) . $ <eq:intro-joint>]

For a finite-dimensional $alpha \|_(C_d)$-projective representation $sigma_d$, define

$ H \( d \, sigma_d \) = E \( A_d \, B_d \, delta_d \, tilde(sigma)_d \) \, #h(2em) tilde(sigma)_d \( a \) = sigma_d \( a \, delta_d \( a \) \) . $

Here $delta_d : A_d arrow.r B_d$ is the isomorphism whose graph is $C_d$. Section 3 realizes $H \( d \, sigma_d \)$ as the orthogonal sum of copies of the representation space indexed by $\( Gamma times Lambda \) \/ C_d$, and describes the induced transports between them.

The full stabilizer matters because a graph presentation can split one of these fibres into several pieces. If $C_delta = { \( a \, delta \( a \) \) : a in A }$ represents $d$, the finite set $C_d \/ C_delta$ indexes the pieces that must be grouped together. Their projective representation is induced from $C_delta$ to $C_d$ with multiplier $alpha$. Bimodule projections may mix these pieces; they become fibre projections only after this grouping.

#prodef([Theorem 1.4.])[
  (All finite correspondences).  #emph[Let $Gamma in cal(K)$, let $Lambda$ be any countably infinite ICC group, and let $mu$ and $omega$ be arbitrary normalized scalar cocycles on these groups.]
  + #emph[Every nonzero bifinite $L_mu \( Gamma \)$–$L_omega \( Lambda \)$ correspondence is a closed bimodule summand of a finite direct sum of the models in Equation (1.3).]
  + #emph[Every such correspondence has a finite orthogonal decomposition into full-germ orbit bundles. After choosing distinct germ orbits and allowing zero fibres, all bounded bimodule maps are 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.5)")
 $ "Hom"_(M upright("–") N) #h(-0.16667em) (xor.big_d H \( d \, sigma_d \) \, xor.big_d H \( d \, tau_d \)) = xor.big_d "Hom"_(C_d \, alpha) \( sigma_d \, tau_d \) . $ <eq:intro-hom>]

 Norm, adjoint, and composition are fibrewise. Projection summands are exactly projective-representation summands in the fibres. A correspondence $K = xor.big_d H \( d \, sigma_d \)$ has dimensions 
$ dim_M K = sum_d dim_(bb(C)) \( sigma_d \) \[ Lambda : B_d \] \, #h(2em) dim K_N = sum_d dim_(bb(C)) \( sigma_d \) \[ Gamma : A_d \] . $

 Both are positive integers when $K eq.not 0$.]
  + #emph[Conjugation and Connes fusion correspond to conjugation and balanced tensor product of these bundles. They are equivalently computed by finite double-coset induction. The scalar transports are given explicitly in Equations (3.4) and (3.17) and Section 3.]
] <thm:correspondences>

The integer dimensions in part (ii) are the dimensions of the actual correspondence, including a proper summand of a presenting model. That distinction is essential to the stable recovery argument: the two dimensions of an equivalence correspondence are reciprocal, so here both must equal one. The projective data throughout concern only the matched ratio in Equation (1.2); neither individual endpoint cocycle is assumed finite type.

Theorems 2 and 3 will follow from this map-level classification in Section 4. These are structural classifications; they do not assert a decision procedure for arbitrary group presentations or cocycle classes.

For untwisted factors, the same results identify the outer automorphism group and recover the group from its reduced C\*-algebra whenever that algebra has a unique tracial state. Section 5 also gives commensurability and finite-type neighbor criteria, named lattice examples, and the finite-index endomorphism consequence for $L \( upright("SL")_3 \( bb(Z) \) \)$. The last consequence uses a separate classical covolume argument.

=== 1.5 History and the role of the arithmetic category <history-and-the-role-of-the-arithmetic-category>

Popa’s deformation/rigidity theory produced rigidity theorems for crossed products and Bernoulli actions. Ioana’s $W^(*)$-superrigidity theorem for Bernoulli actions of ICC property-$\( T \)$ groups concerns an action together with its crossed product #cite(label("Ioana2011")). Ioana–Popa–Vaes obtained the first $W^(*)$-superrigid groups, in classes of generalized wreath products #cite(label("IPV2013")). Chifan–Ioana–Osin–Sun constructed the first such groups with property $\( T \)$, using wreath-like products #cite(label("CIOS2023")). Rigidity of an action and recovery from a bare group factor are distinct problems, and the correspondence category asks additionally for finite index, multiplicities, and composition.

The geometric route for lattices has its own antecedents. For lattices in $"Sp" \( n \, 1 \) \/ { plus.minus 1 }$, $n gt.eq 2$, Cowling–Haagerup’s completely bounded approximation invariant already determines $n$ from the group factor #cite(label("CowlingHaagerup1989")) Main theorem and Propositions 6.1–6.2. Bekka classified the characters of $upright("SL")_n \( bb(Z) \)$ for $n gt.eq 3$, and Peterson proved a finite-factor representation dichotomy for irreducible higher-rank property-$\( T \)$ lattices #cite(label("Bekka2007")) #cite(label("Peterson2014")). Boutonnet–Houdayer developed stationary and noncommutative boundary methods and recovered character rigidity in that framework #cite(label("BoutonnetHoudayer2021")). These results provide important context for the companion’s geometry. They do not by themselves identify all finite correspondences to arbitrary ICC groups. The companion proves the exhaustion input in the full mixed-local-field class stated here, with reducible products and arbitrary scalar twists.

Jones’s index and the expectation inequality of Pimsner–Popa provide the dimension and positivity framework #cite(label("Jones1983")) #cite(label("PimsnerPopa1986")). We use Connes’s correspondence category and relative tensor product #cite(label("Connes1994")) Chapter V, Appendix B. Complete finite-index classifications and explicit fusion rules already occur in Vaes’s work on generalized Bernoulli crossed products #cite(label("Vaes2008")) Theorem 2.2 and Section 6.2. Theorem 2.2 allows scalar twists at both endpoints and a finite-dimensional projective representation of their matched ratio; Definition 6.8 and Theorem 6.9 supply projective induction and the cocycle-corrected double-coset rule. We adapt this calculus to graph bundles for twisted group factors. The full stabilizer of a virtual-isomorphism germ identifies every bounded intertwiner and every selecting projection, including projections that mix smaller presenting fibres.

Donvil–Vaes prove rigidity under arbitrary scalar twists and virtual isomorphism for certain left-right wreath products #cite(label("DonvilVaes2025")). Their Proposition 6.5 reconstructs an intermediate factor from finite-dimensional homogeneous spaces and their actual products, and Theorem 6.6 applies this construction to arbitrary bifinite factor neighbors. The source cocycle is already arbitrary there, and the conclusion retains a finite-type ratio of the two cocycles. We adapt their homogeneous-product and minimal-corner argument from automorphism degrees to a grading by virtual automorphism germs of $Gamma$; finite-index domains and boundedness of the homogeneous vectors are treated explicitly. The companion supplies the distinct exhaustion theorem for the local-field lattice class. The finite-type ascent used for an untwisted source is the projective-induction statement of #cite(label("DonvilVaes2025")) Lemmas 6.7–6.8.

Chifan–Fernández Quero–Osin–Tan obtain stable canonical recovery for scalar twists of certain property-$\( T \)$ wreath-like products when the source cocycle is trivial on the abelian base #cite(label("CFOT2026")) Theorem F. Their comparison group is arbitrary ICC. The unrestricted source cocycles and lattice class treated here enter through the companion’s distinct exhaustion theorem.

Outside the property-$\( T \)$ setting, the nonisomorphic free groups $bb(F)_2$ and $bb(F)_3$ satisfy $L \( bb(F)_2 \) tilde.equiv L \( bb(F)_3 \)$ #cite(label("OpenAIFreeGroupFactors2026")) Theorem 1.2. These factors have fundamental group $bb(R)_(> 0)$ #cite(label("OpenAIFreeGroupFactors2026")) Corollary 6.1, in contrast to the trivial fundamental groups in Theorem 2.

=== 1.6 Proof strategy and navigation <proof-strategy-and-navigation>

There are three algebraic steps. First, the graph models admit orthogonal finite-dimensional fibres indexed by graph cosets. Fibres with the same virtual-isomorphism germ must be grouped together before passing to a summand. The full stabilizer of a germ has only one finite orbit on the germ space. Square summability of each column of a bounded intertwiner therefore forces that map to preserve each grouped fibre. This proves the Hom formula and makes the two dimensions sums of integer fibre ranks times subgroup indices.

Second, a bifinite correspondence with an unknown factor produces a finite-index extension of the known group factor. Arithmetic exhaustion grades its $L^2$ space by finite-dimensional germ fibres. The Pimsner–Popa inequality turns those vectors into bounded operators. Products can then be computed on a common subgroup domain, and they multiply the germ degrees. In a minimal corner of the identity fibre, every nonzero degree is a unitary line. Those lines, with their actual products and trace, recover the group and scalar cocycle. The identity block supplies the projective representation of the cocycle ratio.

Third, the equivalence correspondence of a specified stable isomorphism has reciprocal dimensions. Their integrality forces both to be one, leaving one full graph and one one-dimensional projective fibre. The correspondence identification recovers the cochain. The column realizations in the given matrix corners then recover the implementing partial isometry for the specified map. Figure 1 separates these two consequences of the category calculation.

