Section 2 records the relative tensor convention and exact companion interface. Section 3 proves all model formulas, then applies exhaustion. Section 4 reconstructs factor neighbors and specified stable maps. Section 5 proves the further consequences described above.

== 2 Finite modules and the exhaustion interface <sec:setup>

Our algebraic calculations need only countability and ICC. The geometric class enters at one precise point: the exhaustion statement below says that the explicit models contain every bifinite correspondence. We first fix the norm convention for their construction.

For completeness, $L_mu \( Gamma \)$ is a factor when $Gamma$ is ICC: the absolute values of the Fourier coefficients of a central $L^2$ vector are constant on conjugacy classes, so square summability kills every nonidentity coefficient.

=== 2.1 Relative tensor products and elementary dimensions <relative-tensor-products-and-elementary-dimensions>

All Hilbert-space inner products are linear in the second variable. A vector $xi$ in an $M$–$N$ correspondence is #emph[right bounded] if $R_xi hat(a) = xi a$ extends to a bounded map from $L^2 \( N \)$. For an $N$–$P$ correspondence, the relative tensor product is the completion, after quotienting out zero-norm vectors, of the tensors with right-bounded first vector and inner product

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.1)")
 $ chevron.l xi ⊗_N eta \, xi' ⊗_N eta' chevron.r = chevron.l eta \, \( R_xi^(*) R_(xi') \) eta' chevron.r . $ <eq:relative-inner-product>]

The right-module map $R_xi^(*) R_(xi')$ is left multiplication by an element of $N$. Balancing means $\( xi a \) ⊗_N eta = xi ⊗_N \( a eta \)$ for $a in N$. This is the finite tracial form of Connes’s relative tensor product #cite(label("Connes1994")) Chapter V, Appendix B, Theorems 13 and 20.

A finite right $N$-module has a column realization $p L^2 \( N \)^n$, with dimension $\( "Tr"_n ⊗ tau_N \) \( p \)$. The vectors in $p N^n$ are right bounded and dense. The projection model and its dimension are independent of the chosen realization #cite(label("Jones2009")) Theorem 10.1.1 and Proposition 10.1.3. We prove the normality of the graph models directly in Lemmas 7 and 8. The same elementary construction precedes the geometric argument in the companion, so its exhaustion proof has no dependence on our all-maps or summand classification. For $n = dim sigma$, the elementary dimensions are $n \[ Lambda : B \]$ and $n \[ Gamma : A \]$ in left/right order. The dimensions of #emph[actual summands] will require the full-stabilizer calculation.

=== 2.2 The geometric input <the-geometric-input>

The real simple factors in Definition 1 have higher rank or are locally $"Sp" \( n \, 1 \)$, $n gt.eq 2$, or $F_(4 \( - 20 \))$. At a finite place their relative rank is at least two. Complex places are treated as real Lie groups in differential arguments. Property $\( T \)$ passes between a locally compact group and a lattice and between a discrete group and a finite-index subgroup; see #cite(label("BHV2008")). In particular, that class is closed under abstract commensurability among countably infinite ICC groups. The geometric companion treats arbitrary lattices in the permitted finite products, without a dense-projection hypothesis. We use exactly the following theorem.

#prodef([Proposition 2.1.])[
  (Arithmetic exhaustion, companion Theorem 1.2).  #emph[Let $Gamma in cal(K)$, let $Lambda$ be any countably infinite ICC group, and let $mu \, omega$ be arbitrary normalized scalar cocycles. Every nonzero separable bifinite $L_mu \( Gamma \)$–$L_omega \( Lambda \)$ correspondence is a closed bimodule summand of a finite direct sum of the models $E \( A \, B \, delta \, sigma \)$ in Equation (1.3), with the exact multiplier in Equation (1.2).]
] <prop:recovery-exhaustion>

This is the Arithmetic exhaustion theorem of the complete companion #cite(label("ArithmeticCompanion")) Theorem 1.2. Its conclusion retains actual finite-index subgroup isomorphisms and their exact scalar ratios. We do not assume that the projective representations have finite image, that either cocycle is finite type, or that the original correspondence is irreducible. The algebraic proofs that follow are independent of this proposition until its explicit application at the end of Section 3.

== 3 Full-germ correspondence calculus <sec:calculus>

The calculations in this section are algebraic. We give a group-factor version of the projective induction and cocycle-corrected double-coset calculus in Vaes’s work on generalized Bernoulli crossed products #cite(label("Vaes2008")) Theorem 2.2 and Section 6.2, especially Definition 6.8 and Theorem 6.9. We prove the formulas here, including the full-stabilizer argument for every bounded intertwiner and every bimodule summand. Until we apply Proposition 5, the groups need only be countable and ICC; the geometric hypotheses of Definition 1 are not used. We first construct the arithmetic models with their exact scalar transports, and then determine their intertwiners, summands, conjugates, and relative tensor products. This order also verifies that every model appearing in the exhaustion is a normal bifinite correspondence.

Write

$ M = L_mu \( Gamma \) \, #h(2em) N = L_omega \( Lambda \) \, #h(2em) D = Gamma times Lambda \, #h(2em) alpha \( \( g \, h \) \, \( g' \, h' \) \) = mu \( g \, g' \) overline(omega \( h \, h' \)) . $

On an $M$–$N$ correspondence the joint group action is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.1)")
 $ U_(\( g \, h \)) xi = u_g xi v_h^(*) . $ <eq:calculus-joint-action>]

It has multiplier $alpha$. Indeed, its rightmost product is $v_(h')^(*) v_h^(*) = \( v_h v_(h') \)^(*)$, so $U_(\( g \, h \)) U_(\( g' \, h' \)) = mu \( g \, g' \) overline(omega \( h \, h' \)) U_(\( g g' \, h h' \))$. In particular the two coordinate actions commute exactly.

=== 3.1 Germs and their full stabilizers <germs-and-their-full-stabilizers>

A germ from $Gamma$ to $Lambda$ is an isomorphism $delta : A arrow.r B$ between finite-index subgroups, modulo agreement after restriction to a common finite-index subgroup. Composition means composition on a finite-index domain where both representatives are defined. For example, if $epsilon : C arrow.r F$ is a representative from $Lambda$ to a third group, then $epsilon delta$ is defined on $delta^(- 1) \( B ∩ C \)$ and has image $epsilon \( B ∩ C \)$. Both have finite index in the relevant endpoint group. Restricting representatives further does not change the germ, and a common further restriction verifies associativity. Inversion is represented by the inverse subgroup isomorphism.

For a germ $d$ from $Gamma$ to $Lambda$, let

$ \( g \, h \) d = "Ad" \( h \) thin d thin "Ad" \( g^(- 1) \) \, #h(2em) C_d = { \( g \, h \) in D : \( g \, h \) d = d } . $

If $delta : A arrow.r B$ represents $d$, denote its graph by $C_delta = { \( a \, delta \( a \) \) : a in A }$. We distinguish this graph from the full stabilizer $C_d$.

#prodef([Lemma 3.1.])[
  (Full stabilizers and the finite-orbit test).  #emph[The maps $g mapsto \[ "Ad" \( g \) \]$ and $h mapsto \[ "Ad" \( h \) \]$ are injective. The group $C_d$ is the graph of an isomorphism $delta_d : A_d arrow.r B_d$ between finite-index subgroups. It contains $C_delta$ at finite index, and $delta_d$ extends $delta$. If a finite-index subgroup of $C_d$ fixes another germ $x$ from $Gamma$ to $Lambda$, then $x = d$. Equivalently, the only finite $C_d$-orbit in the space of these germs is ${ d }$. The same conclusion holds with $C_d$ replaced by $C_delta$.]
] <lem:calculus-germ-test>

#proof[
  The centralizer of a finite-index subgroup of an ICC group is trivial: an element of that centralizer has a centralizer of finite index and therefore a finite conjugacy class. This proves injectivity of the inner-germ maps.
  The stabilizer condition can be written
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.2)")
   $ "Ad" \( h \) = d "Ad" \( g \) d^(- 1) . $ <eq:calculus-stabilizer-relation>]
  If either coordinate of an element of $C_d$ is the identity, injectivity of the inner-germ maps forces the other coordinate to be the identity. Thus both coordinate projections on $C_d$ are injective. Their images $A_d$ and $B_d$ form the domain and range of a group isomorphism $delta_d$. The inclusion $C_delta subset C_d$ follows by evaluating Equation (3.2) on a common finite-index domain. Consequently $A subset A_d$, $B subset B_d$, and $delta_d \|_A = delta$. Both projected groups have finite index, and $\[ C_d : C_delta \] = \[ A_d : A \] < oo$.
  Suppose $C_0 lt.eq C_d$ has finite index and fixes $x$. Its first projection $A_0$ has finite index in $Gamma$. For every $\( a \, b \) in C_0$ the two stabilizer equations give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.3)")
   $ x "Ad" \( a \) x^(- 1) = "Ad" \( b \) = d "Ad" \( a \) d^(- 1) . $ <eq:calculus-finite-orbit-test>]
  Choose representatives $chi$ and $delta$ for $x$ and $d$, and restrict $a$ to $A_0 ∩ "dom" \( chi \) ∩ "dom" \( delta \)$. On this finite-index subgroup the outer expressions in Equation (3.3) are the inner germs of $chi \( a \)$ and $delta \( a \)$. Injectivity in $Lambda$ gives $chi \( a \) = delta \( a \)$, whence $x = d$. A finite orbit has a finite-index point stabilizer, so this proves the equivalent orbit statement. Finally, a finite-index subgroup of $C_delta$ also has finite index in $C_d$. ◻
]

The finite-orbit conclusion will make germ labels intrinsic to the induced models. Cosets of $C_d$ correspond to actual germs, whereas several cosets of a presenting graph may represent the same germ. After grouping those cosets, a bounded intertwiner will have matrix blocks between the resulting finite-dimensional fibres. Equivariance makes a block’s norm constant along a stabilizer orbit. The lemma will therefore force such a map to preserve each germ label, once square summability of its columns is used.

=== 3.2 Projective induction and the arithmetic model <projective-induction-and-the-arithmetic-model>

Projective induction, induction in stages, and double-coset tensor decompositions belong to Mackey’s representation-theoretic framework #cite(label("Mackey1958")) Theorems 4.1, 4.2, and 4.6. The following coordinates fix the multiplier throughout and prove every scalar identity used here.

Let $C lt.eq D$ be a subgroup and let $gamma$ be a normalized unitary projective representation on a finite-dimensional space $V$, with multiplier $alpha \|_(C times C)$. Write $X = D \/ C$ for left cosets. Choose representatives $r_x in D$, with $r_C = e$, and give each $x in X$ a copy $V_x$ of $V$. Set $cal(H) \( C \, gamma \) = xor.big_(x in X) V_x$, with the counting Hilbert norm. For $s in D$ put

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.4)")
 $ c \( s \, x \) & = r_(s x)^(- 1) s r_x \,\
U_s^X \( x \) & = frac(alpha \( s \, r_x \), alpha \( r_(s x) \, c \( s \, x \) \)) thin gamma \( c \( s \, x \) \) : V_x arrow.r V_(s x) . $ <eq:input-A1>]

#prodef([Lemma 3.2.])[
  (Exact projective induction).  #emph[Equation (3.4) defines a unitary projective representation of $D$ with multiplier $alpha$. Changing the coset representatives gives a unitarily equivalent bundle by unitary identifications of its individual fibres. If $C$ is the graph of a finite-index subgroup isomorphism $A arrow.r B$, the bundle has commuting normal $M$ and right $N$ actions, and is bifinite.]
] <lem:calculus-induction>

#proof[
  The correction $c \( s \, x \)$ belongs to $C$, and
  $ c \( s \, t x \) c \( t \, x \) = c \( s t \, x \) . $
  Using the cocycle identity for $alpha$ and the projective multiplication of $gamma$ in Equation (3.4) gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.5)")
   $ U_s^X \( t x \) U_t^X \( x \) = alpha \( s \, t \) U_(s t)^X \( x \) . $ <eq:calculus-transport-identity>]
  One can also check all the scalar factors at once in the central extension with multiplication $\( z \, s \) \( w \, t \) = \( z w alpha \( s \, t \) \, s t \)$: the representation of its preimage of $C$ is $\( z \, c \) mapsto z gamma \( c \)$, and the equality $s r_x = r_(s x) c \( s \, x \)$ gives exactly the ratio in Equation (3.4). Thus this argument retains, rather than discards, the fixed multiplier.
  For explicit independence of representatives, suppose $r'_x = r_x k_x$, where $k_x in C$. The identification from the new coordinate copy of $V_x$ to the old one is
  $ F_x = alpha \( r_x \, k_x \)^(- 1) gamma \( k_x \) . $
  Indeed identifying a fibre by $U_(r'_x)$ instead of $U_(r_x)$ has precisely this effect. Equation (3.5) then gives $F_(s x) U' zws_s^X \( x \) = U_s^X \( x \) F_x$.
  Now assume that $C$ is a graph. Both coordinate groups act freely on $X$. For example, if $\( g \, e \) r_x C = r_x C$, then $r_x^(- 1) \( g \, e \) r_x in C$ has second coordinate $e$, so $g = e$. On an orbit of the first coordinate group choose a fibre $V_(x_0)$. The unitary identification
  $ ell^2 \( Gamma \) ⊗ V_(x_0) arrow.r xor.big_(g in Gamma) V_(\( g \, e \) x_0) \, #h(2em) delta_g ⊗ xi mapsto U_(\( g \, e \))^X xi $
  intertwines its action with $lambda_mu ⊗ 1$, since $U_(\( a \, e \)) U_(\( g \, e \)) = mu \( a \, g \) U_(\( a g \, e \))$. Hence it extends normally to $L_mu \( Gamma \)$. The same argument for the second coordinate gives copies of $lambda_(macron(omega))$, which are the standard normal representations of the generators acting by right adjoints $xi mapsto xi v_h^(*)$. The two actions commute, because the product cocycle has trivial mixed-coordinate factors.
  The first coordinate orbits are indexed by $Lambda \/ B$, and the second coordinate orbits by $Gamma \/ A$. They are finite in number. Each is a regular module with multiplicity $dim_(bb(C)) V$, proving bifiniteness as well as the dimension formulas below. ◻
]

We next identify this construction with the Connes-product model in Theorem 4. This also supplies a direct normality check of the subgroup action used to define that product.

#prodef([Lemma 3.3.])[
  (The graph-bundle unitary).  #emph[Let $delta : A arrow.r B$ be a finite-index subgroup isomorphism and let $sigma : A arrow.r cal(U) \( V \)$ have multiplier $beta = mu \|_(A times A) \/ delta^(*) omega$. Define $gamma \( a \, delta \( a \) \) = sigma \( a \)$. Then 
$ E \( A \, B \, delta \, sigma \) = L^2 \( M \) macron(⊗)_(L_(mu \| A) \( A \)) \( V ⊗ L^2 \( N \) \) tilde.equiv cal(H) \( C_delta \, gamma \) . $

 The unitary sends the vector $xi$ in the fibre labelled by $x$ to $U_(r_x) j xi$, where $j xi = hat(1) ⊗ \( xi ⊗ hat(1) \)$ in the displayed model.]
] <lem:calculus-graph-model>

#proof[
  On $V ⊗ L^2 \( N \)$ the subgroup action is
  $ u_a \( xi ⊗ eta \) = sigma \( a \) xi ⊗ v_(delta \( a \)) eta . $
  Its multiplier is $beta \( a \, b \) omega \( delta \( a \) \, delta \( b \) \) = mu \( a \, b \)$. For normality, choose representatives $t$ for $B without Lambda$. On the summand spanned by $xi ⊗ v_(delta \( a \)) v_t$, the map
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.6)")
   $ xi ⊗ v_(delta \( a \)) v_t mapsto delta_a ⊗ sigma \( a \)^(*) xi quad upright("in ") ell^2 \( A \) ⊗ V $ <eq:calculus-absorption>]
  is unitary and conjugates the subgroup action to $lambda_(mu \| A) ⊗ 1$. To check the phase, applying $u_b$ before this map produces
  $ omega \( delta \( b \) \, delta \( a \) \) sigma \( b a \)^(*) sigma \( b \) = omega \( delta \( b \) \, delta \( a \) \) beta \( b \, a \) sigma \( a \)^(*) = mu \( b \, a \) sigma \( a \)^(*) . $
  Thus the left subgroup action is normal. Its right $N$ action is the standard action on the second factor and commutes with it.
  Choose representatives $r$ for $Gamma \/ A$. As a right $L_(mu \| A) \( A \)$ module, $L^2 \( M \)$ has orthonormal free basis $u_r$; the assertion follows directly from $E_(L_(mu \| A) \( A \)) \( u_r^(*) u_(r') \) = delta_(r \, r') 1$. The relative product therefore identifies with the orthogonal sum
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.7)")
   $ E \( A \, B \, delta \, sigma \) = xor.big_(r in Gamma \/ A) u_r ⊗ \( V ⊗ L^2 \( N \) \) . $ <eq:calculus-coset-model>]
  At its identity fibre the graph action is exactly
  $ U_(\( a \, delta \( a \) \)) j xi = u_a ⊗ \( xi ⊗ v_(delta \( a \))^(*) \) = j sigma \( a \) xi . $
  For $g = r a$, balancing in Equation (3.7) gives the more explicit formula
  $ U_(\( g \, h \)) j xi = mu \( r \, a \)^(- 1) u_r ⊗ #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] sigma \( a \) xi ⊗ v_(delta \( a \)) v_h^(*) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
  The last Fourier coordinate is $delta \( a \) h^(- 1)$, up to its displayed unit scalar. Thus two translated identity fibres are orthogonal unless their labels in $D \/ C_delta$ agree: equality of their first cosets and last Fourier coordinates is exactly that coset equality. Conversely these fibres exhaust every summand in Equation (3.7). They are an orthogonal, transitive system with graph action $gamma$ at the basepoint. Finally
  $ U_s U_(r_x) j = frac(alpha \( s \, r_x \), alpha \( r_(s x) \, c \( s \, x \) \)) U_(r_(s x)) j gamma \( c \( s \, x \) \) \, $
  which proves that the stated unitary has exactly the transport of Equation (3.4). ◻
]

For a full stabilizer we write

$ H \( d \, gamma \) = cal(H) \( C_d \, gamma \) . $

Its labels identify with actual germs in the orbit $D d$. There is also a canonical regrouping of an original graph model over this full-germ orbit. The map $D \/ C_delta arrow.r D \/ C_d$ has finite fibres. Its fibre above $C_d$ carries

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.8)")
 $ "Ind"_(C_delta)^(C_d \, alpha) gamma \, $ <eq:calculus-regrouping>]

where projective induction uses Equation (3.4) with $D$ replaced by $C_d$ and with the restricted cocycle. Indeed that fibre is the sum indexed by $C_d \/ C_delta$, and its transports are precisely that formula. Regrouping the orthogonal sum consequently gives

$ cal(H) \( C_delta \, gamma \) tilde.equiv H #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] d \, "Ind"_(C_delta)^(C_d \, alpha) gamma #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $

This description includes the phases of finite induction; it does not replace the projective representation by an ordinary one.

Changing an orbit representative also retains a scalar transport. If $d' = k d$, then $C_(d') = k C_d k^(- 1)$. Identify the new base fibre with the old one using $U_k$. For $c' = k c k^(- 1) in C_(d')$ the representation in these coordinates is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.9)")
 $ gamma' \( c' \) = frac(alpha \( c' \, k \), alpha \( k \, c \)) thin gamma \( c \) . $ <eq:calculus-representative-change>]

Indeed $U_(c') U_k = alpha \( c' \, k \) alpha \( k \, c \)^(- 1) U_k U_c$. This gives the exact representative-change equivalence, including its restricted multiplier. Two choices of $k$ leading to the same germ give equivalent fibres by the stabilizer action itself.

#prodef([Proposition 3.4.])[
  (Dimensions of the actual orbit modules).  #emph[For a finite-dimensional $alpha \|_(C_d)$-projective representation $gamma$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.10)")
 $ dim_M H \( d \, gamma \) = dim_(bb(C)) \( gamma \) \[ Lambda : B_d \] \, #h(2em) dim H \( d \, gamma \)_N = dim_(bb(C)) \( gamma \) \[ Gamma : A_d \] . $ <eq:calculus-full-dimensions>]

 For the original model these formulas read 
$ dim_M E \( A \, B \, delta \, sigma \) = dim_(bb(C)) \( sigma \) \[ Lambda : B \] \, #h(2em) dim E \( A \, B \, delta \, sigma \)_N = dim_(bb(C)) \( sigma \) \[ Gamma : A \] . $

 Zero fibres may be omitted. Dimensions add under finite orthogonal sums.]
] <prop:calculus-dimensions>

#proof[
  The free coordinate-orbit identifications in Lemma 7 exhibit exactly the stated numbers of copies of the standard left and right modules. Each standard module has dimension one for the normalized factor trace. Lemma 8 gives the original-model formulas. ◻
]

=== 3.3 All bounded intertwiners and all summands <all-bounded-intertwiners-and-all-summands>

#prodef([Theorem 3.5.])[
  (Full-germ intertwiner formula).  #emph[Choose finitely many distinct $D$-orbits of germs and a representative $d$ in each orbit. Let $gamma_d \, tau_d$ be finite-dimensional projective representations of $C_d$, with multiplier $alpha \|_(C_d)$; either may be zero. Then 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.11)")
 $ "Hom"_(M upright("–") N) (xor.big_d H \( d \, gamma_d \) \, thin xor.big_d H \( d \, tau_d \)) = xor.big_d "Hom"_(C_d \, alpha) \( gamma_d \, tau_d \) . $ <eq:calculus-hom>]

 The equality is implemented by restriction to the representative fibres and extension by transport. Norm is the maximum of the fibre norms, and adjoints and composition are fibrewise. In particular all closed bimodule summands are obtained by taking invariant subspaces of these finite-dimensional fibre representations.]
] <thm:calculus-hom>

#proof[
  Realize both modules over their union of full-germ orbits, assigning a zero fibre where necessary. An intertwiner $T$ has finite-dimensional matrix blocks $T_(y x) : V_x arrow.r W_y$. If $\( e_i \)$ is an orthonormal basis of $V_x$, then
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.12)")
   $ sum_y parallel T_(y x) parallel_(upright("HS"))^2 = sum_i parallel T e_i parallel^2 lt.eq dim \( V_x \) parallel T parallel^2 < oo . $ <eq:calculus-hs-column>]
  Equivariance identifies $T_(s y \, s x)$ with $U_s^tau \( y \) T_(y x) U_s^gamma \( x \)^(*)$. Its Hilbert–Schmidt norm is therefore unchanged. For $s in C_x$, Equation (3.12) implies that a nonzero block $T_(y x)$ can occur only when the $C_x$-orbit of $y$ is finite. By Lemma 6, this forces $y = x$. Thus every intertwiner is diagonal in the germ labels, including between initially different orbit bundles.
  At a representative label $d$, equivariance is exactly the requirement that $T_d$ intertwine the two $alpha \|_(C_d)$-projective representations. Conversely, such an operator extends by
  $ T_(s d) = U_s^tau \( d \) T_d U_s^gamma \( d \)^(*) . $
  This is independent of the choice of $s$: changing $s$ by an element of $C_d$ uses the intertwining identity, and the identical scalar multiplier on both sides cancels. The extension is bounded with norm $parallel T_d parallel$ on that orbit and commutes with every joint group generator. Normality of the endpoint representations then makes it a bimodule intertwiner. This proves Equation (3.11) and the norm assertion. The formulas for adjoint and composition follow on each fibre.
  A closed bimodule summand is the range of a projection in the bounded bimodule commutant. The preceding description identifies that projection with a family of $C_d$-equivariant fibre projections. Their ranges are precisely the asserted projective-representation summands, and their transported ranges give the entire module summand. ◻
]

Consider any closed bimodule summand $K$ of a finite sum of the original graph models. Regroup each model by Equation (3.8), combine copies of the same full-germ orbit, and apply Theorem 10 to the projection onto $K$. We obtain a finite orthogonal sum of full-germ bundles, with exactly the intertwiners in Equation (3.11). In particular, if its fibre representations are $rho_d$, its actual dimensions are

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.13)")
 $ dim_M K = sum_d dim_(bb(C)) \( rho_d \) \[ Lambda : B_d \] \, #h(2em) dim K_N = sum_d dim_(bb(C)) \( rho_d \) \[ Gamma : A_d \] . $ <eq:calculus-summand-dimensions>]

Both are positive integers for $K eq.not 0$. This conclusion uses the description of its projection; the dimensions of a containing model alone would not imply it.

#prodef([Remark 3.6.])[
  (Why a presenting graph must be enlarged).  Let $M = N = L_mu \( Gamma \)$, with arbitrary $mu$, and suppose $A < Gamma$ has finite index $k > 1$. Present the identity germ $d$ by $"id"_A : A arrow.r A$, with the one-dimensional trivial fibre. The multiplier on every diagonal pair is $mu \( a \, b \) overline(mu \( a \, b \)) = 1$, so this is a model for any source twist. Its full stabilizer is the diagonal copy of $Gamma$. Regrouping therefore replaces the presenting fibre by the permutation representation on $ell^2 \( Gamma \/ A \)$.
  The constant vectors form an invariant line. Its orthogonal projection has every matrix entry equal to $1 \/ k$, and selects $H \( d \, 1 \) = L^2 \( M \)$ from the model $E \( A \, A \, "id"_A \, 1 \)$. The selected module has dimensions $\( 1 \, 1 \)$, whereas the containing model has dimensions $\( k \, k \)$. On each of the $k$ original one-dimensional fibres above $d$, the diagonal block of the selecting projection is $1 \/ k$, which is not a projection. Grouping those fibres at the full stabilizer is what exposes the invariant line and its actual rank.
]

=== 3.4 Conjugation <conjugation>

On the conjugate correspondence $overline(cal(H) \( C \, gamma \))$ the endpoint groups are swapped. Its subgroup and base fibre are

$ C^(upright("op")) = { \( h \, g \) : \( g \, h \) in C } \, #h(2em) macron(gamma) \( h \, g \) macron(xi) = overline(gamma \( g \, h \) xi) . $

The multiplier is $omega times.square macron(mu)$, since $macron(gamma)$ conjugates $alpha$ and swaps its two variables. The label $x$ of a full-germ bundle becomes the inverse germ $x^(- 1)$: indeed

$ #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] "Ad" \( h \) x "Ad" \( g^(- 1) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]^(- 1) = "Ad" \( g \) x^(- 1) "Ad" \( h^(- 1) \) . $

With swapped coset representatives, the transport is explicitly

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.14)")
 $ U_(\( h \, g \))^(macron(X)) \( macron(x) \) = overline(U_(\( g \, h \))^X \( x \)) . $ <eq:calculus-conjugate-transport>]

Thus it includes the conjugate of the scalar ratio in Equation (3.4). At the module level this equality is the defining identity $v_h accent(xi u, ‾)_g^(*) = overline(u_g xi v_h^(*))$. For an original graph $delta : A arrow.r B$, the new graph is $delta^(- 1) : B arrow.r A$ and its fibre representation is $b mapsto overline(sigma \( delta^(- 1) \( b \) \))$. Fibre intertwiners are conjugated by the same rule; their adjoints and norms are preserved. Proposition 9 also shows directly that the left and right dimensions are exchanged.

=== 3.5 Balanced fusion and its scalar transport <balanced-fusion-and-its-scalar-transport>

Let $P = L_nu \( Pi \)$. Consider a bundle $cal(H)_X$ from $\( Gamma \, mu \)$ to $\( Lambda \, omega \)$ and a bundle $cal(H)_Y$ from $\( Lambda \, omega \)$ to $\( Pi \, nu \)$, each constructed as above. Write $V_x$ and $W_y$ for their fibres and $alpha' = omega times.square macron(nu)$. Finite direct sums are handled by distribution, so we may first take one transitive bundle at each endpoint. Define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.15)")
 $ Z = \( X times Y \) \/ Lambda \, #h(2em) h \( x \, y \) = #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( 1 \, h \) x \, \( h \, 1 \) y #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <eq:calculus-balanced-labels>]

Identify the tensor fibres along this action using

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.16)")
 $ J_h \( x \, y \) = U_(\( 1 \, h \))^X \( x \) ⊗ U_(\( h \, 1 \))^Y \( y \) . $ <eq:calculus-middle-transport>]

These identifications are genuine unitary group transports, because their multiplier is $overline(omega \( h_1 \, h_2 \)) omega \( h_1 \, h_2 \) = 1$. The action on labels is free, as already its action on $X$ is free. Each quotient fibre $V_z$ is consequently unambiguously a tensor fibre after choosing one representative of $z$. Put $cal(H)_Z = xor.big_(z in Z) V_z$, with counting norm at any such representatives. Changing representatives gives a unitary identification by Equation (3.16).

The outer transport before passage to the quotient is $U_(\( g \, 1 \))^X ⊗ U_(\( 1 \, k \))^Y$. It commutes exactly with every $J_h$: each mixed-coordinate factor of either product cocycle is one. Its multiplier is $mu times.square macron(nu)$. For an explicit formula choose a representative $\( x \, y \)$ of $z$ and representatives for all quotient labels. There is a unique $h$ such that

$ #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( g \, h \) x \, \( h \, k \) y #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] $

is the chosen representative of $\( g \, k \) z$. With $s = \( g \, h \)$, $t = \( h \, k \)$, $c = c_X \( s \, x \)$ and $b = c_Y \( t \, y \)$, the quotient transport is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.17)")
 $ frac(alpha \( s \, r_x \) alpha' \( t \, r_y \), alpha \( r_(s x) \, c \) alpha' \( r_(t y) \, b \)) gamma_X \( c \) ⊗ gamma_Y \( b \) . $ <eq:input-A2>]

It is the product of the two transports in Equation (3.4). There is no additional factor from the compensating element $h$, since its lift is the genuine transport in Equation (3.16).

To attach a germ to a quotient label, first recall the map from graph cosets to germs. For $x = \( g \, h \) C_delta in X$, put

$ d_x = "Ad" \( h \) \[ delta \] "Ad" \( g^(- 1) \) \, $

and define $d_y$ in the same way for $y in Y$. These are the graph-coset-to-germ maps from the earlier regrouping, applied at their respective endpoints; they need not be injective. The middle action changes the two associated germs by

$ \( d_x \, d_y \) mapsto \( "Ad" \( h \) d_x \, thin d_y "Ad" \( h^(- 1) \) \) . $

Hence $z = \[ \( x \, y \) \] mapsto d_y d_x$ is well defined. Distinct quotient labels can still have the same germ; the next proposition explains how their fibres are regrouped.

#prodef([Proposition 3.7.])[
  (Fusion as a counting tensor-fibre bundle).  #emph[There is a unitary correspondence identification 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.18)")
 $ cal(H)_X macron(⊗)_N cal(H)_Y tilde.equiv cal(H)_Z $ <eq:calculus-fusion-unitary>]

 under which outer transport is Equation (3.17). The space $Z$ has finitely many outer-group orbits. For each $z = \[ \( x \, y \) \]$, its stabilizer is a finite-index subgroup-isomorphism graph with germ $d_y d_x$. Regrouping these orbits over full stabilizers by Equation (3.8) gives the full-germ decomposition of the fusion.]
] <prop:calculus-fusion>

#proof[
  First we check the Hilbert norm and the relative product explicitly. Every $xi in V_x$ is right bounded: the map
  $ R_xi : L^2 \( N \) arrow.r cal(H)_X \, #h(2em) hat(n) mapsto xi n $
  initially defined on the group algebra extends with norm $parallel xi parallel$. Indeed the vectors $xi v_h$ occupy distinct fibres as $h$ varies, and all have norm $parallel xi parallel$. Choose one $x_i$ in each right $Lambda$-orbit of $X$, and an orthonormal basis $\( e_(i j) \)_j$ of $V_(x_i)$. Orthogonality of all their group translates gives
  $ R_(e_(i j))^(*) R_(e_(i' j')) = delta_(i i') delta_(j j') 1 . $
  Their ranges exhaust $cal(H)_X$, because the translates exhaust every labelled fibre. They therefore form an orthonormal free right $N$ basis, with finitely many elements.
  The defining inner product of the relative tensor product now gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.19)")
   $ ⟨e_(i j) ⊗_N eta \, e_(i' j') ⊗_N eta'⟩ = delta_(i i') delta_(j j') chevron.l eta \, eta' chevron.r . $ <eq:calculus-relative-inner-product>]
  Thus that product is unitarily $xor.big_i \( V_(x_i) ⊗ cal(H)_Y \)$. Every label in $Z$ has a unique representative with first coordinate one of the fixed $x_i$: freeness of the right action on $X$ gives uniqueness of the required balancing element. Consequently the counting tensor-fibre Hilbert sum $cal(H)_Z$ is exactly this same orthogonal sum. Its map to the relative product sends $xi ⊗ eta in V_x ⊗ W_y$ to $xi ⊗_N eta$. Equation (3.19) proves that it is isometric and onto. It is independent of the representative, since
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.20)")
   $ \( xi v_h^(*) \) ⊗_N \( v_h eta \) = xi ⊗_N \( v_h^(*) v_h eta \) = xi ⊗_N eta . $ <eq:calculus-balanced-vector>]
  Applying the outer generators to these vectors proves Equation (3.17) at the correspondence level.
  For finiteness and the stabilizers we give the explicit double-coset calculation, which also specifies the composed germ and its fibre representation. Write the two graph maps as
  $ delta : A arrow.r B lt.eq Lambda \, #h(2em) epsilon : C arrow.r F lt.eq Pi \, $
  with $A lt.eq Gamma$ and $C lt.eq Lambda$ of finite index, and take $X = \( Gamma times Lambda \) \/ C_delta$, $Y = \( Lambda times Pi \) \/ C_epsilon$. Choose $m$ in the finite double-coset set $B without Lambda \/ C$. Represent its outer orbit by
  $ z_m = \[ \( C_delta \, \( m \, 1 \) C_epsilon \) \] . $
  Outer translation and balancing send an arbitrary pair to the form
  $ \( \( g \, h \) C_delta \, \( ell \, k \) C_epsilon \) mapsto \( C_delta \, \( h^(- 1) ell \, 1 \) C_epsilon \) . $
  Changing the initial coset representatives replaces $h^(- 1) ell$ by $delta \( a \)^(- 1) h^(- 1) ell c$ for $a in A$, $c in C$. Hence these outer orbits are indexed exactly by $B without Lambda \/ C$.
  An outer element $\( a \, p \)$ stabilizes $z_m$ precisely when some $h$ satisfies
  $ \( a \, h \) in C_delta \, #h(2em) \( m^(- 1) h m \, p \) in C_epsilon . $
  Its stabilizer is consequently the graph of
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.21)")
   $ A_m & = delta^(- 1) \( B ∩ m C m^(- 1) \) \,\
theta_m & = epsilon compose "Ad" \( m^(- 1) \) compose delta : A_m arrow.r F_m \,\
F_m & = epsilon \( C ∩ m^(- 1) B m \) . $ <eq:calculus-composed-graph>]
  Both projections have finite index. In particular
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.22)")
   $ Z tilde.equiv union.sq.big_(m in B without Lambda \/ C) \( Gamma times Pi \) \/ C_(theta_m) . $ <eq:calculus-double-coset-labels>]
  Here $C_(theta_m)$ denotes the graph of the displayed actual map, not necessarily its full-germ stabilizer. At $z_m$ the composite germ is $\[ epsilon "Ad" \( m^(- 1) \) delta \] = \[ theta_m \]$. Its graph lies at finite index in its full stabilizer by Lemma 6. This proves all the stated regrouping assertions. ◻
]

For completeness, the last proof yields an explicit finite double-coset formula without unspecified stabilizer representations. Let $sigma$ and $tau$ be the original fibre representations on $A$ and $C$. In the fibre at $z_m$ choose $r_(\( m \, 1 \) C_epsilon) = \( m \, 1 \)$. For $a in A_m$, write $b = delta \( a \)$ and $c = m^(- 1) b m in C$. Equation (3.17), with compensating element $h = b$, gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.23)")
 $ gamma_m \( a \) = q_m \( b \) thin sigma \( a \) ⊗ tau \( m^(- 1) b m \) \, #h(2em) q_m \( b \) = frac(omega \( b \, m \), omega \( m \, m^(- 1) b m \)) . $ <eq:calculus-double-coset-fibre>]

The correction has the exact coboundary

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.24)")
 $ frac(q_m \( b \) q_m \( b' \), q_m \( b b' \)) = frac(omega \( b \, b' \), omega \( m^(- 1) b m \, m^(- 1) b' m \)) . $ <eq:calculus-conjugation-coboundary>]

To verify this, the twisted group-unit identity $v_b v_m = q_m \( b \) v_m v_(m^(- 1) b m)$ can be multiplied successively for $b \, b'$ and compared with the identity for $b b'$. Combining Equation (3.24) with the multipliers of $sigma$ and $tau$ shows that $gamma_m$ has multiplier $mu \|_(A_m) \/ theta_m^(*) nu$. Hence

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.25)")
 $ E \( A \, B \, delta \, sigma \) macron(⊗)_N E \( C \, F \, epsilon \, tau \) tilde.equiv xor.big_(m in B without Lambda \/ C) E \( A_m \, F_m \, theta_m \, gamma_m \) . $ <eq:calculus-double-coset-fusion>]

The appropriate endpoint algebras in the second model and in each summand are understood from their displayed groups. One then applies finite projective induction to the full stabilizers and combines any equal full-germ orbits. Formula (3.23) exhibits explicitly why replacing the twisted fusion rule by its ordinary version would lose a phase.

The indices in Equation (3.21) satisfy

$ \[ Gamma : A_m \] = \[ Gamma : A \] \[ B : B ∩ m C m^(- 1) \] \, #h(2em) \[ Pi : F_m \] = \[ Pi : F \] \[ C : C ∩ m^(- 1) B m \] . $

Counting the left $B$-orbits in $Lambda \/ C$ and the right $C$-orbits in $B without Lambda$ gives, respectively,

$ sum_m \[ B : B ∩ m C m^(- 1) \] = \[ Lambda : C \] \, #h(2em) sum_m \[ C : C ∩ m^(- 1) B m \] = \[ Lambda : B \] . $

Thus both coordinate groups have finitely many orbits in the fused label space. These formulas also verify, directly from the models and their counting norms, the usual multiplicativity of the left dimensions and of the right dimensions under this fusion.

=== 3.6 Maps, associativity, and completion of the calculus <maps-associativity-and-completion-of-the-calculus>

The preceding identifications respect the entire operator calculus, not only the isomorphism classes of objects. For the map calculation, first regroup the inputs into full-germ bundles. Theorem 10 then represents every bounded intertwiner by an equivariant family of fibre maps. An equivariant family $T_x : V_x arrow.r V'_x$ and an equivariant family $S_y : W_y arrow.r W'_y$ give the fibre maps $T_x ⊗ S_y$. They commute with the middle identifications in Equation (3.16), so descend to the quotient fibres. Under the unitary of Proposition 12, they are precisely $T macron(⊗)_N S$, as is seen on $xi ⊗_N eta$. Their norms are bounded by $parallel T parallel parallel S parallel$; adjoints and compositions agree fibrewise. Regrouping by finite projective induction is a regrouping of these same Hilbert fibres and maps. Conjugation has the map formula already given in Equation (3.14).

For three bundles, both orders of fusion use the quotient of the triple label space by the two middle groups. Their actions commute: on the shared middle bundle they are its commuting coordinate actions, and their projective factors cancel with those in the adjacent bundles. The fibre identification between the two orders is the ordinary unitary

$ \( xi ⊗ eta \) ⊗ zeta mapsto xi ⊗ \( eta ⊗ zeta \) . $

The counting norms and Equation (3.19) show that it is also the canonical associativity unitary for the relative products. All scalar identifications are those of Equation (3.16); hence this assertion requires no change of cocycle. For any further number of factors these maps agree on elementary tensors, which are total, so the associativity identifications are coherent. The unit correspondence $L^2 \( M \)$ is the identity-germ model with its one-dimensional trivial graph representation; its unit maps are ordinary left and right multiplication on the corresponding relative tensors.

Conjugation reverses a balanced product. The unitary

$ overline(cal(H)_X macron(⊗)_N cal(H)_Y) arrow.r overline(cal(H)_Y) macron(⊗)_N overline(cal(H)_X) \, #h(2em) overline(xi ⊗_N eta) mapsto macron(eta) ⊗_N macron(xi) $

is well defined on tensors of vectors in labelled fibres: the simultaneous middle transport becomes the same transport on the swapped conjugate fibres, with both scalar factors conjugated and their product still one. The counting tensor-fibre model of Proposition 12 proves preservation of the norm: the map bijects the quotient labels and is unitary on each conjugate tensor fibre. These tensors are total. It intertwines the swapped endpoint actions and the conjugated fibre maps. Thus the conjugation, unit, and associativity identifications agree with those of the correspondence category at the level of actual operators.

We now apply the geometric input. Proposition 5 places every bifinite correspondence with source in $cal(K)$ inside a finite sum of graph models. The preceding full-germ calculation resolves its selecting projection into fibre projections. Together with the operation formulas just proved, this establishes every assertion of Theorem 4, including all bounded intertwiners, actual summands and their dimensions, and the specified conjugation and fusion maps. Throughout the section only the matched-subgroup ratio is required to have a finite-dimensional projective realization. None of the calculations imposes a finite-image condition on that representation or a finite-type condition on either individual endpoint cocycle.

== 4 Arbitrary factor endpoints and stable canonical recovery <sec:endpoints>

We now use the correspondence classification to recover the multiplication of an unknown factor. The argument first realizes that factor, up to amplification, as a finite-index extension of the given twisted group factor. Its self-correspondence grading then consists of bounded operators and respects the actual product. This additional multiplication step is what turns the correspondence data into a group presentation. The homogeneous-product and minimal-corner construction has a direct predecessor in Donvil–Vaes #cite(label("DonvilVaes2025")) Proposition 6.5 and Theorem 6.6. Their construction already permits arbitrary source cocycles and recovers the exact matched ratio. We adapt it from globally defined automorphism degrees to finite-index isomorphism germs.

=== 4.1 A finite-index extension and its intrinsic grading <a-finite-index-extension-and-its-intrinsic-grading>

#prodef([Lemma 4.1.])[
  (From a bifinite neighbor to an extension).  #emph[Let $M \, P$ be $upright("II")_1$ factors and let $zws_M K_P eq.not 0$ be bifinite. The commutant $Q$ of the right $P$-action is a positive finite amplification of $P$, and the given left action identifies $M$ with a unital finite-index subfactor of $Q$.]
] <lem:endpoints-extension>

#proof[
  The finite right module has a column realization $K = p L^2 \( P \)^n$, with $r = \( "Tr"_n ⊗ tau_P \) \( p \) = dim K_P > 0$. Its right-action commutant is $Q = p \( M_n \( bb(C) \) accent(⊗ P, ‾) \) p = P^r$, equipped with the normalized corner trace. The left action of $M$ is a normal unital embedding into $Q$, because a nonzero representation of a factor has zero kernel. Its restriction preserves normalized trace.
  As a left $Q$-module, $K$ has finite positive dimension $1 \/ r$. Choose an integer $j$ with $j \/ r gt.eq 1$. Comparison of finite module projections embeds $L^2 \( Q \)$ as a left $Q$-submodule of $K^(xor j)$. Restriction to $M$ makes it a finite left $M$-module. Thus the Jones index $\[ Q : M \] = dim_M L^2 \( Q \)$ is finite. This uses the standard finite-factor column and index identifications; see #cite(label("Jones1983")) and #cite(label("Jones2009")) Theorem 10.1.1, Proposition 10.1.3, and Theorem 10.2.1. ◻
]

Fix $Gamma in cal(K)$, an arbitrary normalized scalar cocycle $mu$, and $M = L_mu \( Gamma \)$. Write $D = "Comm" \( Gamma \)$ for the group of virtual automorphism germs defined in Section 3. The map

$ iota : Gamma arrow.r D \, #h(2em) iota \( g \) = \[ "Ad" \( g \) \] \, $

is injective: an element in its kernel centralizes a finite-index subgroup of $Gamma$, and hence has a finite conjugacy class. For a representative $delta : A arrow.r B$ of $d in D$, one has

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.1)")
 $ d thin iota \( a \) thin d^(- 1) = iota \( delta \( a \) \) #h(2em) \( a in A \) . $ <eq:endpoints-inner-germs>]

#prodef([Lemma 4.2.])[
  (The intrinsic self-correspondence grading).  #emph[Every bifinite $M$–$M$ correspondence has an orthogonal decomposition 
$ K = xor.big_(d in D) K_d $

 into finite-dimensional spaces, with only countably many nonzero terms, such that 
$ u_g K_d u_h^(*) = K_(iota \( g \) d iota \( h \)^(- 1)) . $

 The support is contained in finitely many $iota \( Gamma \)$-double cosets. For every representative $delta : A arrow.r B$ of $d$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.2)")
 $ K_d = {xi in K : dim_(bb(C)) "span" { u_(delta \( a \)) xi u_a^(*) : a in A } < oo} . $ <eq:input-B1>]

 In particular, for the fixed presentation $M = L_mu \( Gamma \)$, the grading is determined by the two actions and does not depend on a choice of arithmetic model decomposition.]
] <lem:endpoints-grading>

#proof[
  Apply Theorem 4 and the full-germ decomposition of Section 3. Use the inverse of its germ labels, so that our degree is a germ from the right group to the left group. If $x$ is an old label and $d = x^(- 1)$, the old action gives
  $ #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] "Ad" \( h \) x "Ad" \( g^(- 1) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]^(- 1) = iota \( g \) d iota \( h \)^(- 1) . $
  Combining the finitely many representation fibres at each identical inverse label gives $K_d$ and the stated covariance. The finitely many old germ orbits become finitely many $iota \( Gamma \)$-double cosets. Separability makes the nonzero support countable.
  For a representative $delta$, the operators $U_a xi = u_(delta \( a \)) xi u_a^(*)$ preserve $K_d$. Their multiplier is $mu \( delta \( a \) \, delta \( b \) \) overline(mu \( a \, b \))$. They form a projective unitary representation, so its orbit span is invariant, even when the multiplier is not trivial. This proves the forward inclusion in Equation (4.2).
  Conversely let $W$ be a finite-dimensional invariant orbit span and choose an orthonormal basis $xi_1 \, dots.h \, xi_l$ of $W$. If $P_x$ projects onto $K_x$, the numbers
  $ m \( x \) = sum_(i = 1)^l parallel P_x xi_i parallel^2 $
  have finite sum $l$ and are invariant under $x mapsto iota \( delta \( a \) \) x iota \( a \)^(- 1)$. Indeed $U_a$ permutes the degree spaces and carries the chosen basis of $W$ to another orthonormal basis. Positive mass is therefore possible only on a finite orbit. The stabilizer of such an $x$ contains a finite-index subgroup $A_0 lt.eq A$, and
  $ x iota \( a \) x^(- 1) = iota \( delta \( a \) \) #h(2em) \( a in A_0 \) . $
  Represent $x$ by $epsilon.alt : C arrow.r E$ and restrict to $A_0 ∩ C$. Equation (4.1) and injectivity of $iota$ give $epsilon.alt \( a \) = delta \( a \)$ there. Thus $x = d$. All of $W$ lies in $K_d$, proving the converse and the asserted intrinsic description. ◻
]

=== 4.2 Bounded homogeneous operators and the minimal corner <bounded-homogeneous-operators-and-the-minimal-corner>

Let $M subset Q$ now be a unital finite-index inclusion of $upright("II")_1$ factors and apply Lemma 14 to $zws_M L^2 \( Q \)_M$. Until the next lemma, its degree spaces are only Hilbert subspaces of $L^2 \( Q \)$.

#prodef([Lemma 4.3.])[
  (Boundedness and multiplication).  #emph[Every $K_d$ consists of bounded elements of $Q$, and 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.3)")
 $ K_d K_f subset K_(d f) \, #h(2em) K_d^(*) = K_(d^(- 1)) \, #h(2em) 1 in K_1 \, #h(2em) u_a in K_(iota \( a \)) . $ <eq:endpoints-grade-product>]

 Consequently $K_1$ is a finite-dimensional unital $*$-subalgebra of $Q$, normalized by the $u_a$, and $tau_Q \( K_d \) = 0$ for $d eq.not 1$.]
] <lem:endpoints-bounded-grades>

#proof[
  Let $xi in K_d$. For distinct $g in Gamma$, the right translates $xi u_g$ have distinct degrees $d iota \( g \)$. Injectivity of $iota$ therefore gives $chevron.l xi \, xi u_g chevron.r = 0$ for $g eq.not e$. Initially put $a = xi^(*) xi in L^1 \( Q \)_(+)$. The $L^1$ extension of the trace expectation satisfies
  $ E_M \( a \) = parallel xi parallel_2^2 1 : $
  indeed, for each $g in Gamma$,
  $ tau_M \( E_M \( a \) u_g \) = tau_Q \( a u_g \) = chevron.l xi \, xi u_g chevron.r . $
  These values agree with those of $parallel xi parallel_2^2 1$. They determine the normal functional associated to $E_M \( a \) in L^1 \( M \)$, because the group algebra is ultraweakly dense in $M$. For the bounded spectral truncations $a_n = min \( a \, n \)$, Pimsner–Popa’s finite-index inequality #cite(label("PimsnerPopa1986")) Proposition 2.1 gives
  $ \[ Q : M \]^(- 1) a_n lt.eq E_M \( a_n \) lt.eq E_M \( a \) = parallel xi parallel_2^2 1 . $
  Monotone convergence makes $a$ bounded, and hence $xi in Q$, with $parallel xi parallel_oo lt.eq \[ Q : M \]^(1 \/ 2) parallel xi parallel_2$. This argument uses orthogonality of the degrees, so the scalar phases in the right action do not change it. We now verify the product itself.
  Take $xi in K_d$, $eta in K_f$, and representatives $delta : A arrow.r B$, $epsilon.alt : C arrow.r E$. On the finite-index domain $C_0 = { a in C : epsilon.alt \( a \) in A }$, the exact identity
  $ u_(delta epsilon.alt \( a \)) xi eta u_a^(*) = \( u_(delta epsilon.alt \( a \)) xi u_(epsilon.alt \( a \))^(*) \) \( u_(epsilon.alt \( a \)) eta u_a^(*) \) $
  puts the orbit of the product into the finite-dimensional span of products of bases of $K_d$ and $K_f$. These are bounded elements, as just proved. The intrinsic test (4.2) yields $xi eta in K_(d f)$. Taking adjoints in the same test gives $K_d^(*) subset K_(d^(- 1))$, and taking adjoints again gives equality. The vector $1$ is fixed by the identity graph. For the graph of $"Ad" \( a \)$, the vector $u_a$ is carried to a scalar multiple of itself, since the corresponding product of group labels is $a$. Thus its graph orbit span is one-dimensional, even with the twist. This proves Equation (4.3).
  Finite dimension of $K_1$ comes from the grading lemma, and normalization follows from its covariance. Orthogonality to $1 in K_1$ gives the trace assertion for other degrees. ◻
]

Compressing $K_1$ to a scalar algebra will now force each surviving degree to be a unitary line.

#prodef([Lemma 4.4.])[
  (The minimal identity corner).  #emph[Let $p$ be a minimal projection of $K_1$. There are a countable subgroup $Lambda lt.eq D$ and a normalized scalar cocycle $omega$ such that the actual corner $p Q p$, with normalized trace, is isomorphic to $L_omega \( Lambda \)$. Each nonzero space $p K_d p$ is one-dimensional and has a unitary basis vector in the corner.]
] <lem:endpoints-minimal-corner>

#proof[
  Left and right multiplication by $p$ are commuting orthogonal projections on $L^2 \( Q \)$. They preserve each degree by Equation (4.3); their product projects onto $L^2 \( p Q p \)$. Thus, up to the common normalization factor of the corner trace,
  $ L^2 \( p Q p \) = xor.big_(d in D) p K_d p \, #h(2em) p K_1 p = bb(C) p . $
  For $0 eq.not x in p K_d p$, the product and adjoint laws give $x^(*) x = alpha p$ and $x x^(*) = beta p$, with positive scalars. Traciality gives $alpha = beta$. Therefore $x \/ sqrt(alpha)$ is a unitary in $p Q p$. If $y$ belongs to the same degree, $x^(*) y in bb(C) p$, so $y$ is a scalar multiple of $x$.
  Set $Lambda = { d : p K_d p eq.not 0 }$. It contains the identity, is closed under inverses, and is closed under products because products of its homogeneous unitaries cannot vanish. It is countable by orthogonality and separability. Choose unitary vectors $v_d in p K_d p$, with $v_1 = p$, and define
  $ v_d v_f = omega \( d \, f \) v_(d f) . $
  Unitarity makes the coefficient unimodular, associativity gives the 2-cocycle identity, and $v_1 = p$ gives normalization. The $v_d$ form an orthonormal basis for the normalized corner trace. The unitary $delta_d mapsto v_d$ from $ell^2 \( Lambda \)$ to $L^2 \( p Q p \)$ intertwines twisted left regular multiplication with left multiplication by these units.
  Let $Q_0$ be the von Neumann algebra they generate. The trace-preserving expectation onto $Q_0$ has $L^2$-range containing every $v_d$, so it has full range and $Q_0 = p Q p$. The basis unitary therefore gives a normal unital complex-linear $*$-isomorphism $L_omega \( Lambda \) tilde.equiv p Q p$, as claimed. ◻
]

=== 4.3 The ratio condition and the reconstructed group <the-ratio-condition-and-the-reconstructed-group>

The minimal corner has supplied a twisted group algebra, but the group $Lambda$ still needs to satisfy the hypotheses of Theorem 3. We next recover a finite-index copy of a subgroup of $Gamma$ inside $Lambda$, obtain the projective ratio from the identity matrix block, and prove that $Lambda$ is ICC.

#prodef([Proposition 4.5.])[
  (Arithmetic constraints on the corner).  #emph[For the corner in Lemma 16, the group $Lambda$ is countably infinite and ICC. There are finite-index subgroups $A lt.eq Gamma$, $B lt.eq Lambda$, an actual group isomorphism $delta : A arrow.r B$, and a finite-dimensional projective representation with multiplier $mu \|_A \/ delta^(*) \( omega \|_B \)$.]
] <prop:endpoints-corner-constraints>

#proof[
  Let $z$ be the central support of $p$ in the finite-dimensional algebra $K_1$, so that $z K_1$ is the matrix block containing $p$. We first split the action on this block from the homogeneous unitaries that will survive in $p Q p$. The subgroup
  $ A = { a in Gamma : u_a z u_a^(*) = z } $
  has finite index, because conjugation permutes the finitely many central blocks. Although these conjugations preserve $z$, they may move $p$ inside its matrix block. We therefore remove their action on that block before compressing. Choose unitary matrix implementers $s_a in cal(U) \( z K_1 \)$, $s_e = z$, for $"Ad" \( u_a \) \|_(z K_1)$. Since these automorphisms form an ordinary group action, there is a normalized scalar cocycle $kappa$ with
  $ s_a s_b = kappa \( a \, b \) s_(a b) . $
  These matrices already constitute a finite-dimensional projective representation. Their image is allowed to be infinite.
  The unitaries $q_a = s_a^(*) u_a z in z Q z$ commute with $z K_1$. Using $u_a s_b^(*) u_a^(*) = s_a s_b^(*) s_a^(*)$, their product is
  $ q_a q_b & = s_a^(*) \( u_a s_b^(*) u_a^(*) \) u_a u_b z\
 & = mu \( a \, b \) s_b^(*) s_a^(*) u_(a b) z = mu \( a \, b \) overline(kappa \( a \, b \)) q_(a b) . $
  They are homogeneous of degree $iota \( a \)$, and they commute with $p$. Their compressions $t_a = p q_a p$ are therefore corner unitaries in $p K_(iota \( a \)) p$, satisfying the same product formula. It follows that $B = iota \( A \)$ lies in $Lambda$, and that $delta = iota \|_A : A arrow.r B$ is an actual isomorphism.
  To compare their multiplier with the cocycle already chosen in Lemma 16, write the basis unitary of degree $iota \( a \)$ as $v_(iota \( a \)) = lambda \( a \) t_a$, where $lambda : A arrow.r bb(T)$ and $lambda \( e \) = 1$. With the convention
  $ \( partial lambda \) \( a \, b \) = lambda \( a \) lambda \( b \) lambda \( a b \)^(- 1) \, $
  we obtain
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.4)")
   $ delta^(*) omega = \( partial lambda \) mu \|_A thin overline(kappa) \, #h(2em) frac(mu \|_A, delta^(*) omega) = \( partial lambda \)^(- 1) kappa . $ <eq:endpoints-ratio>]
  On the defining space of the block, the matrices $a mapsto overline(lambda \( a \)) s_a$ realize precisely the second multiplier. This is the required ratio condition.
  We have the subgroup isomorphism and its exact ratio. It remains to show that its image has finite index in $Lambda$ and that $Lambda$ is ICC. Put $H = iota \( Gamma \)$. Every $d in D$ commensurates $H$, by Equation (4.1) on a finite-index domain. Consequently each double coset $H d H$ is a finite union of right $H$-cosets. Lemma 14 puts the original degree support in finitely many such double cosets. Since $B$ has finite index in $H$, that support is contained in finitely many right $B$-cosets. The subgroup $Lambda$ is contained in this support and contains $B$; hence $\[ Lambda : B \] < oo$. In particular it is infinite.
  Suppose $l in Lambda$ has finite conjugacy class. Its centralizer $C_Lambda \( l \)$ has finite index in $Lambda$. Thus $A_0 = iota^(- 1) \( C_Lambda \( l \) ∩ B \)$ has finite index in $Gamma$, and $l$ centralizes $iota \( A_0 \)$. Represent its germ by $epsilon.alt : C arrow.r E$. For $a in A_0 ∩ C$, centralization and Equation (4.1) give $iota \( epsilon.alt \( a \) \) = iota \( a \)$. Injectivity of $iota$ gives $epsilon.alt \( a \) = a$ on this finite-index subgroup. Thus $l = 1$ in $D$, proving ICC. The construction uses only subgroup isomorphisms and introduces no finite kernel. ◻
]

#proof[
  Apply Lemma 13 to the given nonzero bifinite correspondence. It gives a finite-index extension $M subset Q = P^r$. The preceding lemmas and Proposition 17 give $p Q p tilde.equiv L_omega \( Lambda \)$ with the asserted group and ratio data. Since $p Q p$ is the amplification $P^(r tau_Q \( p \))$, standard column equivalences give
  $ P tilde.equiv L_omega \( Lambda \)^t \, #h(2em) t = \( r tau_Q \( p \) \)^(- 1) > 0 . $
  Conversely the stated subgroup and projective data give a nonzero bifinite arithmetic correspondence from $M$ to $L_omega \( Lambda \)$, by Section 3. Compose it with the corner equivalence from that factor to its amplification and then with the specified factor isomorphism to $P$. This is a nonzero bifinite $M$–$P$ correspondence.
  When $mu = 1$, conjugating the finite-dimensional ratio representation and transporting it through $delta$ gives a representation with multiplier $omega \|_B$. Projective induction from $B$ to $Lambda$, with the given global multiplier $omega$, is finite-dimensional and realizes $omega$ itself; compare #cite(label("DonvilVaes2025")) Lemmas 6.7–6.8. The induction formula of Section 3 applies equally to this finite-index subgroup. For general $mu$ the construction proves only the exact ratio in Equation (4.4), as required. ◻
]

=== 4.4 Stable scales and the actual isomorphism <stable-scales-and-the-actual-isomorphism>

For a specified stable isomorphism, the category gives a different constraint: its equivalence correspondence has reciprocal dimensions. After integrality forces both dimensions to be one, we will use the given corner columns to identify the specified map. All dimensions below use the normalized factor traces and the ordinary unnormalized matrix trace $"Tr"_n$, with $"Tr"_n \( 1 \) = n$.

#prodef([Lemma 4.6.])[
  (Dimension and corner equivalences).  #emph[For composable bifinite correspondences of finite factors, left dimensions multiply under relative tensor product, and so do right dimensions. If $M^t = p \( M_n \( bb(C) \) accent(⊗ M, ‾) \) p$ and $C_p = p L^2 \( M \)^n$, then $zws_(M^t) C_p zws_M$ is an equivalence correspondence with dimensions $\( 1 \/ t \, t \)$ in left/right order.]
] <lem:endpoints-equivalence-dimensions>

#proof[
  For the right-dimension assertion, realize a right $N$-module $H$ as $e L^2 \( N \)^k$. Then $H ⊗_N K = e K^k$. The normalized trace on the finite factor $"End" \( K_P \)$ restricts to the normalized trace on its unital copy of $N$. Computing the dimension of $e K^k$ therefore gives
  $ dim \( H ⊗_N K \)_P = \( "Tr"_k ⊗ tau_N \) \( e \) thin dim K_P . $
  The left formula follows by conjugating the correspondences. The column $C_p$ has right dimension $t$ by its defining projection. The standard column pairings identify $C_p ⊗_M overline(C_p)$ with $L^2 \( M^t \)$ and $overline(C_p) ⊗_(M^t) C_p$ with $L^2 \( M \)$. The multiplication formula makes its left dimension $1 \/ t$. These identifications use the normalized corner trace, which accounts for the reciprocal scale. ◻
]

#proof[
  Set $M = L_omega \( Lambda \)$ and $N = L_nu \( Pi \)$. Use the actual given corner presentations
  $ M^t = p M_n \( M \) p \, #h(2em) N^s = q M_m \( N \) q \, $
  and the columns $C_p = p L^2 \( M \)^n$, $C_q = q L^2 \( N \)^m$. A specified isomorphism $theta : M^t arrow.r N^s$ gives the $M$–$N$ equivalence correspondence
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.5)")
   $ H_theta = overline(C_p) ⊗_(M^t) zws_theta L^2 \( N^s \) ⊗_(N^s) C_q . $ <eq:endpoints-lifted-isomorphism>]
  By Lemma 18, its left and right dimensions are $t \/ s$ and $s \/ t$. Theorem 4 applies to $H_theta$ because $Lambda in cal(K)$ and $Pi$ is countably infinite ICC. Its full-germ decomposition and Equation (3.13) make both numbers positive integers. Their product is one, so $t = s$.
  The same formula now forces a single full-germ orbit, a one-dimensional fibre, and both stabilizer projections equal to the whole endpoint groups. Indeed each nonzero orbit contributes its positive integer fibre dimension times a positive integer subgroup index to each of the two sums, both of which equal one. Thus its graph is an actual group isomorphism $delta : Lambda arrow.r Pi$. Consequently $Pi in cal(K)$, since $Lambda in cal(K)$ and this class is invariant under group isomorphism. The one-dimensional fibre is a normalized cochain $b : Lambda arrow.r bb(T)$ with
  $ omega \( g \, h \) = b \( g \) b \( h \) b \( g h \)^(- 1) nu \( delta \( g \) \, delta \( h \) \) . $
  The arithmetic bundle identification in Section 3 therefore gives $H_theta tilde.equiv zws_phi L^2 \( N \)$, where
  $ phi \( u_g \) = b \( g \) v_(delta \( g \)) . $
  Multiplication and the displayed cochain equation define a $*$-homomorphism on the group algebra. Its trace-preserving basis unitary extends it to a normal unital isomorphism $M arrow.r N$.
  We now return to the specified map in its actual corners. First choose any partial isometry between the corresponding projections; then the correspondence identification will supply the inner correction that makes it implement $theta$. The projections $phi^(\( n \)) \( p \)$ and $q$ have equal unnormalized trace $t = s$. Comparison of projections in the finite matrix amplification of $N$ gives a rectangular partial isometry
  $ z in M_(m \, n) \( N \) \, #h(2em) z^(*) z = phi^(\( n \)) \( p \) \, #h(2em) z z^(*) = q . $
  Define $theta_0 \( x \) = z phi^(\( n \)) \( x \) z^(*)$ on $p M_n \( M \) p$. Its lifted correspondence in Equation (4.5) is $zws_phi L^2 \( N \)$. To check this directly, on bounded columns set $F \( xi \) = z phi^(\( n \)) \( xi \)$, applying $phi$ entrywise. For $x in p M_n \( M \) p$, $a in M$, and $xi \, eta in p M^n$, one has
  $ F \( x xi a \) = theta_0 \( x \) F \( xi \) phi \( a \) \, #h(2em) F \( xi \)^(*) F \( eta \) = phi \( xi^(*) eta \) . $
  Moreover $F$ is onto the bounded columns $q N^m$, since $z z^(*) = q$. These identities, followed by the normalized column pairings of Lemma 18, contract the two column factors to the trace module with left action $phi$.
  Consequently $H_theta tilde.equiv H_(theta_0)$. Tensor on the left with $C_p$ and on the right with $overline(C_q)$; the column equivalences cancel and give a unitary
  $ U : zws_theta L^2 \( N^s \) arrow.r zws_(theta_0) L^2 \( N^s \) . $
  A right-linear unitary on this standard trace module is left multiplication by a unitary $w in N^s$. Its left intertwining identity says $w theta \( x \) = theta_0 \( x \) w$. Replacing $z$ by $w^(*) z$, which has the same initial and final projections, therefore yields the actual formula
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.6)")
   $ theta \( x \) = z phi^(\( n \)) \( x \) z^(*) #h(2em) \( x in p M_n \( M \) p \) . $ <eq:endpoints-actual-corner-map>]
  At ordinary unit scale this is precisely $theta \( u_g \) = z thin b \( g \) v_(delta \( g \)) z^(*)$ with $z in N$ unitary.
  Conversely a group isomorphism and normalized cochain satisfying the cocycle equation construct $phi$ by the same basis unitary. At equal scales, comparison of the two actual projections supplies $z$, and Equation (4.6) constructs the corner isomorphism. This proves both directions and the specified-map assertion. Taking identical factor inputs and scales $1 \, t$ shows that every factor in the statement has fundamental group ${ 1 }$. ◻
]

== 5 Commensurability, named lattices, and endomorphisms <sec:examples>

The category calculation gives concise consequences with different mathematical content. Untwisted correspondence existence detects abstract commensurability. Stable isomorphism recovers an entire group and a specified map. A finite-index endomorphism requires one additional classical covolume argument; its two module dimensions are not initially reciprocal.

=== 5.1 Untwisted correspondences and finite-type neighbors <untwisted-correspondences-and-finite-type-neighbors>

#prodef([Corollary 5.1.])[
  #emph[Let $Gamma in cal(K)$ and let $Lambda$ be any countably infinite ICC group. There is a nonzero bifinite $L \( Gamma \)$–$L \( Lambda \)$ correspondence if and only if $Gamma$ and $Lambda$ are abstractly commensurable.]
] <cor:commensurability>

#proof[
  A nonzero correspondence has a nonzero full-germ orbit in Theorem 4. Its stabilizer is the graph of an actual isomorphism between finite-index subgroups. Conversely such an isomorphism, with its one-dimensional trivial fibre representation, gives the model in Equation (1.3). ◻
]

With the finite-type convention of Section 1, the endpoint theorem specializes as follows.

#prodef([Corollary 5.2.])[
  #emph[For $Gamma in cal(K)$, a separable $upright("II")_1$ factor $P$ is joined to $L \( Gamma \)$ by a nonzero bifinite correspondence if and only if $P tilde.equiv L_omega \( Lambda \)^t$ for $t > 0$, an ICC group $Lambda$ abstractly commensurable with $Gamma$, and a finite-type cocycle $omega$ on $Lambda$.]
] <cor:untwisted-neighbors>

#proof[
  Theorem 3 and the final projective-induction argument in its proof give necessity. For sufficiency, restrict a projective representation of multiplier $omega$ to the matched subgroup and conjugate it; its multiplier is exactly $1 \/ delta^(*) omega$. The graph model and the amplification equivalence give the required correspondence. ◻
]

For arbitrary source twist this specialization must be replaced by the ratio in Theorem 3. In particular, its direct proof admits the identity correspondence of $L_mu \( Gamma \)$ whatever the individual cocycle $mu$ may be.

=== 5.2 Examples and specified canonical maps <examples-and-specified-canonical-maps>

The familiar arithmetic examples are $upright("SL")_3 \( bb(Z) \)$, its principal congruence subgroup

$ Gamma \( 3 \) = ker #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] upright("SL")_3 \( bb(Z) \) arrow.r upright("SL")_3 \( bb(Z) \/ 3 bb(Z) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \, $

all their finite-index subgroups, $upright("PSL")_n \( bb(Z) \)$ for $n gt.eq 4$, and $upright("PSp")_4 \( bb(Z) \)$, with the latter two symbols denoting the quotient by the center of the integral matrix group. The arithmetic lattice theorem places these projective groups in the corresponding center-free real groups #cite(label("BorelHarishChandra1962")); the finite-covolume statement appears also in #cite(label("BorelHarishChandra1961")) Theorem 1(iv). For even $n$, $upright("PSL")_n \( bb(R) \)$ is the identity root-generated subgroup of $upright("PGL")_n \( bb(R) \)$; for odd $n$ it is all of it. Likewise $upright("PSp")_4 \( bb(R) \)$ is the root-generated adjoint symplectic group. These ambient groups have real rank at least two and property $\( T \)$ #cite(label("BHV2008")).

These projective lattices are ICC. A finite conjugacy class gives a finite-index centralizer. That subgroup is Zariski dense by Borel density, so the element centralizes the connected ambient algebraic group and is trivial in its adjoint form #cite(label("Borel1960")). Equivalently, the centralizer formulation in #cite(label("Furstenberg1976")) Corollary 3 applies directly to the finite-index lattice and gives the ambient center. For $upright("SL")_3 \( bb(Z) \)$ the real center is already trivial. A finite-index subgroup of an ICC group remains ICC: its finite conjugacy class would yield a finite class in the ambient group after using finitely many coset representatives. Thus every group just listed belongs to $cal(K)$.

For any such group $Gamma$, any countably infinite ICC group $Lambda$, and any specified isomorphism

$ theta : L \( Gamma \) arrow.r p M_k \( L \( Lambda \) \) p \, $

Theorem 2 yields

$ \( "Tr"_k ⊗ tau \) \( p \) = 1 \, #h(2em) theta \( u_g \) = chi \( g \) W v_(delta \( g \)) W^(*) \, #h(2em) W^(*) W = 1 \, quad W W^(*) = p \, $

where $W in M_(k \, 1) \( L \( Lambda \) \)$, $delta : Gamma arrow.r Lambda$ is an actual group isomorphism, and $chi : Gamma arrow.r bb(T)$ is a character. This includes the specified column in the specified corner. The character becomes the cochain of Equation (1.1) when scalar twists are present. In particular all the corresponding twisted factors have fundamental group ${ 1 }$.

Definition 1 also includes ICC lattices in the permitted finite-place groups, mixed real and finite-place products, and products with quaternionic or Cayley real factors of property $\( T \)$. Finite products of the preceding discrete examples give reducible cases: a nonidentity element has a nonidentity coordinate with infinite conjugacy class. More generally the companion exhaustion theorem already allows arbitrary reducible lattices, nonuniform lattices, and torsion. No repetition of the individual geometric proofs is required for these examples.

#prodef([Corollary 5.3.])[
  (Outer automorphisms).  #emph[For $Gamma in cal(K)$, there is an isomorphism of abstract groups 
$ "Out" \( L \( Gamma \) \) tilde.equiv "Hom" \( Gamma \, bb(T) \) times.r "Out" \( Gamma \) \, #h(2em) \[ delta \] dot.op chi = chi compose delta^(- 1) \, $

 where $"Out"$ denotes automorphisms modulo inner automorphisms.]
] <cor:outer-automorphisms>

#proof[
  Put $T_chi \( u_g \) = chi \( g \) u_g$ and $U_delta \( u_g \) = u_(delta \( g \))$. These maps extend normally by their trace-preserving basis unitaries. Characters are invariant under inner group automorphisms, and $U_delta T_psi U_delta^(- 1) = T_(psi compose delta^(- 1))$. Thus $\( chi \, \[ delta \] \) mapsto \[ T_chi U_delta \]$ is a well-defined homomorphism for the displayed left action. It is surjective by the untwisted unit-scale case of Theorem 2, whose cochain is then a character.
  For injectivity, suppose $T_chi U_delta = "Ad" \( w \)$, and write $b = chi compose delta$ and $w = sum_x a_x u_x$ in $L^2 \( L \( Gamma \) \)$. The relation
  $ u_(delta \( g \)) w u_g^(- 1) = b \( g \)^(- 1) w $
  shows that $\| a_x \|$ is constant on each orbit of $x mapsto delta \( g \) x g^(- 1)$. A nonzero coefficient has a finite orbit by square summability. Its stabilizer $H$ has finite index, and $delta \( h \) = x h x^(- 1)$ for all $h in H$. Set $beta = "Ad" \( x^(- 1) \) compose delta$ and let $N$ be the normal core of $H$. Since $beta$ fixes $N$ pointwise, $g^(- 1) beta \( g \)$ centralizes $N$ for every $g in Gamma$. The centralizer of a finite-index subgroup is trivial by ICC, as in Lemma 6; hence $delta = "Ad" \( x \)$. Now $v = u_x^(*) w$ implements the pure character twist $b$. Its Fourier-coefficient absolute values are constant on conjugacy classes, so ICC makes $v$ scalar. Therefore $b = 1$, whence $chi = 1$, and $\[ delta \] = 1$, proving injectivity. ◻
]

=== 5.3 Recovery from the reduced group C\*-algebra <recovery-from-the-reduced-group-c-algebra>

Write $C_r^(*) \( Gamma \)$ for the operator-norm closure of the group algebra in the left regular representation.

#prodef([Corollary 5.4.])[
  (Reduced C\*-algebra recovery).  #emph[Let $Gamma in cal(K)$ and suppose that $C_r^(*) \( Gamma \)$ has a unique tracial state. If $Lambda$ is any discrete group and there is a complex-linear $*$-isomorphism $C_r^(*) \( Gamma \) tilde.equiv C_r^(*) \( Lambda \)$, then $Gamma tilde.equiv Lambda$.]
] <cor:reduced-cstar>

#proof[
  Let $alpha : C_r^(*) \( Gamma \) arrow.r C_r^(*) \( Lambda \)$ be the given $*$-isomorphism, and denote the canonical traces by $tau_Gamma$ and $tau_Lambda$. The isomorphism is unital, and uniqueness gives $tau_Lambda compose alpha = tau_Gamma$. Consequently $alpha$ extends isometrically to a unitary between the trace-GNS Hilbert spaces. This unitary intertwines left multiplication, so conjugation by it extends $alpha$ to a normal isomorphism $L \( Gamma \) tilde.equiv L \( Lambda \)$.
  The source reduced algebra is separable. Distinct canonical group unitaries in the target have trace $2$-norm distance $sqrt(2)$, and hence operator-norm distance at least $sqrt(2)$; thus $Lambda$ is countable. Moreover, $L \( Lambda \)$ is an infinite-dimensional factor, so $Lambda$ is infinite and ICC. Indeed, a nonidentity finite conjugacy class would give a nonscalar central class sum. Theorem 2, with both cocycles trivial and both amplification scales equal to one, now gives $Gamma tilde.equiv Lambda$. ◻
]

In particular this applies to $Gamma = upright("SL")_3 \( bb(Z) \)$: its reduced group C\*-algebra has a unique tracial state by #cite(label("Bekka2007")) discussion following Corollary 5.

=== 5.4 Finite-index endomorphisms of the special linear factor <finite-index-endomorphisms-of-the-special-linear-factor>

We state the precise classical fact needed in the final deduction. The finite-index image hypothesis is part of it.

#prodef([Lemma 5.5.])[
  #emph[An injective homomorphism $alpha : upright("SL")_3 \( bb(Z) \) arrow.r upright("SL")_3 \( bb(Z) \)$ whose image has finite index is surjective.]
] <lem:sl3-covolume>

#proof[
  Put $G = upright("SL")_3 \( bb(R) \)$ and $Gamma = upright("SL")_3 \( bb(Z) \)$. The group $G$ is connected, center-free, simple, of real rank two, and has no compact factor. Both $Gamma$ and $alpha \( Gamma \)$ are irreducible lattices in $G$. The classical strong-rigidity theorem extends their isomorphism to an analytic automorphism $Phi$ of $G$; see #cite(label("Prasad1973")) Theorem B. This form of strong rigidity allows nonuniform lattices and torsion; its excluded real rank-one $upright("SL")_2 \( bb(R) \)$ case does not occur here. The theorem combines the uniform rigidity of Mostow with the nonuniform higher-rank work of Margulis and Raghunathan and Prasad’s extension, as explained in that reference.
  The differential $D = d Phi_e$ preserves the nondegenerate Killing form $B$ on the Lie algebra: $D^(sans(T)) B D = B$. Hence $\| det D \| = 1$. A left-invariant Haar density is therefore preserved at the identity and, by translation, everywhere. Consequently
  $ "covol" \( alpha \( Gamma \) \) = "covol" \( Phi \( Gamma \) \) = "covol" \( Gamma \) . $
  On the other hand the first covolume equals $\[ Gamma : alpha \( Gamma \) \] "covol" \( Gamma \)$. The common covolume is positive and finite, so the index equals one. ◻
]

#prodef([Corollary 5.6.])[
  #emph[Every normal unital complex-linear $*$-endomorphism $theta$ of $M = L \( upright("SL")_3 \( bb(Z) \) \)$ with $\[ M : theta \( M \) \] < oo$ is surjective.]
] <cor:sl3-endomorphism>

#proof[
  The endomorphism is injective because its kernel is an ultraweakly closed two-sided ideal in a factor, and it preserves the normalized trace. Equip $H = L^2 \( M \)$ with its usual right action and the left action $x xi = theta \( x \) xi$. It is bifinite, with
  $ dim H_M = 1 \, #h(2em) dim_M H = \[ M : theta \( M \) \] . $
  Its actual full-germ decomposition and Equation (3.13) show that right dimension one leaves exactly one orbit, a one-dimensional fibre, and $A_d = upright("SL")_3 \( bb(Z) \)$. The graph is therefore an isomorphism $delta : upright("SL")_3 \( bb(Z) \) arrow.r B_d$, where $B_d$ has finite index in the same group. Lemma 23 makes $B_d$ the whole group. The left dimension formula now gives $\[ M : theta \( M \) \] = 1$, so $theta \( M \) = M$. ◻
]


